 ## Overview
-Smoothness priors are regularization terms applied during training and inference to reduce surface noise and improve geometric smoothness in neural radiance fields and signed distance fields [S1, unofficial][S2]. They differ from sibling methods like [Background Regularization](13413-background-regularization.md) by explicitly penalizing high-frequency variations in the output fields (opacity, normals, SDF, or scene flow) rather than constraining the background or empty space.
+Smoothness priors are regularization terms applied during training and post-processing to reduce surface noise and improve geometric smoothness in neural radiance fields and signed distance fields [S1, unofficial][S2]. This family builds on [Output Noise Regularization](928-output-noise-regularization.md) and applies distinct loss formulations to enforce smoothness across different geometric representations.
 
 ## Shared principles
-The family applies regularization losses to the output fields to enforce physical plausibility and reduce artifacts. The core mechanism involves penalizing the difference between neighboring values or enforcing alignment with ground-truth geometric cues.
+The family applies regularization losses to output fields to reduce artifacts [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. The core mechanism involves penalizing differences between neighboring values [S2] or enforcing alignment with ground-truth geometric cues [S4].
 
